.Sh DESCRIPTION
This command implements a generic SSL/TLS server which listens for
connections on a given port using SSL/TLS.
.Sh OPTIONS
In addition to the options below, this command also supports the common
and server only options documented
.Xr SSL_CONF_cmd 3
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Sy -help
Print out a usage message.
.It Sy -port Em +int
The TCP port to listen on for connections.
If not specified 4433 is used.
.It Sy -accept Em val
The optional TCP host and port to listen on for connections.
If not specified, *:4433 is used.
.It Sy -unix Em val
Unix domain socket to accept on.
.It Sy -4
Use IPv4 only.
.It Sy -6
Use IPv6 only.
.It Sy -unlink
For -unix, unlink any existing socket first.
.It Sy -context Em val
Sets the SSL context id.
It can be given any string value.
If this option is not present a default value will be used.
.It Sy -verify Em int Ns , Sy -Verify Em int
The verify depth to use.
This specifies the maximum length of the client certificate chain and
makes the server request a certificate from the client.
With the
.Sy -verify
option a certificate is requested but the client does not have to send
one, with the
.Sy -Verify
option the client must supply a certificate or an error occurs.
.Pp
If the cipher suite cannot request a client certificate (for example an
anonymous cipher suite or PSK) this option has no effect.
.Pp
By default, validation of any supplied client certificate and its chain
is done w.r.t.
the (D)TLS Client
.Pf ( Qo Li sslclient Qc )
purpose.
For details see
.Xr openssl-verification-options 1 .
.It Sy -cert Em infile
The certificate to use, most servers cipher suites require the use of a
certificate and some require a certificate with a certain public key
type: for example the DSS cipher suites require a certificate containing
a DSS (DSA) key.
If not specified then the filename
.Pa server.pem
will be used.
.It Sy -cert2 Em infile
The certificate file to use for servername; default is
.Qo Li server2.pem Qc .
.It Sy -certform Sy DER Ns | Ns Sy PEM Ns | Ns Sy P12
The server certificate file format; unspecified by default.
See
.Xr openssl-format-options 1
for details.
.It Sy -cert_chain
A file or URI of untrusted certificates to use when attempting to build
the certificate chain related to the certificate specified via the
.Sy -cert
option.
These untrusted certificates are sent to clients and used for generating
certificate status (aka OCSP stapling) requests.
The input can be in PEM, DER, or PKCS#12 format.
.It Sy -build_chain
Specify whether the application should build the server certificate
chain to be provided to the client.
.It Sy -serverinfo Em val
A file containing one or more blocks of PEM data.
Each PEM block must encode a TLS ServerHello extension (2 bytes type, 2
bytes length, followed by "length" bytes of extension data).
If the client sends an empty TLS ClientHello extension matching the
type, the corresponding ServerHello extension will be returned.
.It Sy -key Em filename Ns | Ns Em uri
The private key to use.
If not specified then the certificate file will be used.
.It Sy -key2 Em filename Ns | Ns Em uri
The private Key file to use for servername if not given via
.Sy -cert2 .
.It Sy -keyform Sy DER Ns | Ns Sy PEM Ns | Ns Sy P12 Ns | Ns Sy ENGINE
The key format; unspecified by default.
See
.Xr openssl-format-options 1
for details.
.It Sy -pass Em val
The private key and certificate file password source.
For more information about the format of
.Em val ,
see
.Xr openssl-passphrase-options 1 .
.It Sy -dcert Em infile Ns , Sy -dkey Em filename Ns | Ns Em uri
Specify an additional certificate and private key, these behave in the
same manner as the
.Sy -cert
and
.Sy -key
options except there is no default if they are not specified (no
additional certificate and key is used).
As noted above some cipher suites require a certificate containing a key
of a certain type.
Some cipher suites need a certificate carrying an RSA key and some a DSS
(DSA) key.
By using RSA and DSS certificates and keys a server can support clients
which only support RSA or DSS cipher suites by using an appropriate
certificate.
.It Sy -dcert_chain
A file or URI of untrusted certificates to use when attempting to build
the server certificate chain when a certificate specified via the
.Sy -dcert
option is in use.
The input can be in PEM, DER, or PKCS#12 format.
.It Sy -dcertform Sy DER Ns | Ns Sy PEM Ns | Ns Sy P12
The format of the additional certificate file; unspecified by default.
See
.Xr openssl-format-options 1
for details.
.It Sy -dkeyform Sy DER Ns | Ns Sy PEM Ns | Ns Sy P12 Ns | Ns Sy ENGINE
The format of the additional private key; unspecified by default.
See
.Xr openssl-format-options 1
for details.
.It Sy -dpass Em val
The passphrase for the additional private key and certificate.
For more information about the format of
.Em val ,
see
.Xr openssl-passphrase-options 1 .
.It Sy -nbio_test
Tests non blocking I/O.
.It Sy -crlf
This option translated a line feed from the terminal into CR+LF.
.It Sy -debug
Print extensive debugging information including a hex dump of all
traffic.
.It Sy -security_debug
Print output from SSL/TLS security framework.
.It Sy -security_debug_verbose
Print more output from SSL/TLS security framework
.It Sy -msg
Show all protocol messages with hex dump.
.It Sy -msgfile Em outfile
File to send output of
.Sy -msg
or
.Sy -trace
to, default standard output.
.It Sy -state
Prints the SSL session states.
.It Sy -CRL Em infile
The CRL file to use.
.It Sy -CRLform Sy DER Ns | Ns Sy PEM
The CRL file format; unspecified by default.
See
.Xr openssl-format-options 1
for details.
.It Sy -crl_download
Download CRLs from distribution points given in CDP extensions of
certificates
.It Sy -verifyCAfile Em filename
A file in PEM format CA containing trusted certificates to use for
verifying client certificates.
.It Sy -verifyCApath Em dir
A directory containing trusted certificates to use for verifying client
certificates.
This directory must be in "hash format", see
.Xr openssl-verify 1
for more information.
.It Sy -verifyCAstore Em uri
The URI of a store containing trusted certificates to use for verifying
client certificates.
.It Sy -chainCAfile Em file
A file in PEM format containing trusted certificates to use when
attempting to build the server certificate chain.
.It Sy -chainCApath Em dir
A directory containing trusted certificates to use for building the
server certificate chain provided to the client.
This directory must be in "hash format", see
.Xr openssl-verify 1
for more information.
.It Sy -chainCAstore Em uri
The URI of a store containing trusted certificates to use for building
the server certificate chain provided to the client.
The URI may indicate a single certificate, as well as a collection of
them.
With URIs in the
.Qo Li file: Qc
scheme, this acts as
.Sy -chainCAfile
or
.Sy -chainCApath ,
depending on if the URI indicates a directory or a single file.
See
.Xr ossl_store-file 7
for more information on the
.Qo Li file: Qc
scheme.
.It Sy -nocert
If this option is set then no certificate is used.
This restricts the cipher suites available to the anonymous ones
(currently just anonymous DH).
.It Sy -quiet
Inhibit printing of session and certificate information.
.It Sy -no_resume_ephemeral
Disable caching and tickets if ephemeral (EC)DH is used.
.It Sy -tlsextdebug
Print a hex dump of any TLS extensions received from the server.
.It Sy -www
Sends a status message back to the client when it connects.
This includes information about the ciphers used and various session
parameters.
The output is in HTML format so this option can be used with a web
browser.
The special URL
.Qo Li /renegcert Qc
turns on client cert validation, and
.Qo Li /reneg Qc
tells the server to request renegotiation.
.It Sy -WWW Ns , Sy -HTTP
Emulates a simple web server.
Pages will be resolved relative to the current directory, for example if
the URL
.Qo Li https://myhost/page.html Qc
is requested the file
.Pa ./page.html
will be sent.
If the
.Sy -HTTP
flag is used, the files are sent directly, and should contain any HTTP
response headers (including status response line).
If the
.Sy -WWW
option is used, the response headers are generated by the server, and
the file extension is examined to determine the
.Sy Content-Type
header.
Extensions of
.Qo Li html Qc ,
.Qo Li htm Qc ,
and
.Qo Li php Qc
are
.Qo Li text/html Qc
and all others are
.Qo Li text/plain Qc .
In addition, the special URL
.Qo Li /stats Qc
will return status information like the
.Sy -www
option.
.It Sy -http_server_binmode
When acting as web-server (using option
.Sy -WWW
or
.Sy -HTTP )
open files requested by the client in binary mode.
.It Sy -no_ca_names
Disable TLS Extension CA Names.
You may want to disable it for security reasons or for compatibility
with some Windows TLS implementations crashing when this extension is
larger than 1024 bytes.
.It Sy -ignore_unexpected_eof
Some TLS implementations do not send the mandatory close_notify alert on
shutdown.
If the application tries to wait for the close_notify alert but the peer
closes the connection without sending it, an error is generated.
When this option is enabled the peer does not need to send the
close_notify alert and a closed connection will be treated as if the
close_notify alert was received.
For more information on shutting down a connection, see
.Xr SSL_shutdown 3 .
.It Sy -servername
Servername for HostName TLS extension.
.It Sy -servername_fatal
On servername mismatch send fatal alert (default: warning alert).
.It Sy -id_prefix Em val
Generate SSL/TLS session IDs prefixed by
.Em val .
This is mostly useful for testing any SSL/TLS code (e.g.
proxies) that wish to deal with multiple servers, when each of which
might be generating a unique range of session IDs (e.g.
with a certain prefix).
.It Sy -keymatexport
Export keying material using label.
.It Sy -keymatexportlen
Export the given number of bytes of keying material; default 20.
.It Sy -no_cache
Disable session cache.
.It Sy -ext_cache .
Disable internal cache, set up and use external cache.
.It Sy -verify_return_error
Verification errors normally just print a message but allow the
connection to continue, for debugging purposes.
If this option is used, then verification errors close the connection.
.It Sy -verify_quiet
No verify output except verify errors.
.It Sy -ign_eof
Ignore input EOF (default: when
.Sy -quiet Ns ).
.It Sy -no_ign_eof
Do not ignore input EOF.
.It Sy -no_ems
Disable Extended master secret negotiation.
.It Sy -status
Enables certificate status request support (aka OCSP stapling).
.It Sy -status_verbose
Enables certificate status request support (aka OCSP stapling) and gives
a verbose printout of the OCSP response.
Use the
.Sy -cert_chain
option to specify the certificate of the server's certificate signer
that is required for certificate status requests.
.It Sy -status_timeout Em int
Sets the timeout for OCSP response to
.Em int
seconds.
.It Sy -proxy Em [http[s]://][userinfo@]host[:port][/path][?query][#fragment]
The HTTP(S) proxy server to use for reaching the OCSP server unless
.Sy -no_proxy
applies, see below.
If the host string is an IPv6 address, it must be enclosed in
.Qo Li [ Qc
and
.Qo Li ] Qc .
The proxy port defaults to 80 or 443 if the scheme is
.Qo Li https Qc ;
apart from that the optional
.Qo Li http:// Qc
or
.Qo Li https:// Qc
prefix is ignored, as well as any userinfo, path, query, and fragment
components.
Defaults to the environment variable
.Qo Li http_proxy Qc
if set, else
.Qo Li HTTP_PROXY Qc
in case no TLS is used, otherwise
.Qo Li https_proxy Qc
if set, else
.Qo Li HTTPS_PROXY Qc .
.It Sy -no_proxy Em addresses
List of IP addresses and/or DNS names of servers not to use an HTTP(S)
proxy for, separated by commas and/or whitespace (where in the latter
case the whole argument must be enclosed in "..."). Default is from the
environment variable
.Qo Li no_proxy Qc
if set, else
.Qo Li NO_PROXY Qc .
.It Sy -status_url Em val
Sets a fallback responder URL to use if no responder URL is present in
the server certificate.
Without this option an error is returned if the server certificate does
not contain a responder address.
The optional userinfo and fragment URL components are ignored.
Any given query component is handled as part of the path component.
.It Sy -status_file Em infile
Overrides any OCSP responder URLs from the certificate and always
provides the OCSP Response stored in the file.
The file must be in DER format.
.It Sy -ssl_config Em val
Configure SSL_CTX using the given configuration value.
.It Sy -trace
Show verbose trace output of protocol messages.
.It Sy -brief
Provide a brief summary of connection parameters instead of the normal
verbose output.
.It Sy -rev
Simple echo server that sends back received text reversed.
Also sets
.Sy -brief .
Cannot be used in conjunction with
.Sy -early_data .
.It Sy -async
Switch on asynchronous mode.
Cryptographic operations will be performed asynchronously.
This will only have an effect if an asynchronous capable engine is also
used via the
.Sy -engine
option.
For test purposes the dummy async engine (dasync) can be used (if
available).
.It Sy -max_send_frag Em +int
The maximum size of data fragment to send.
See
.Xr SSL_CTX_set_max_send_fragment 3
for further information.
.It Sy -split_send_frag Em +int
The size used to split data for encrypt pipelines.
If more data is written in one go than this value then it will be split
into multiple pipelines, up to the maximum number of pipelines defined
by max_pipelines.
This only has an effect if a suitable cipher suite has been negotiated,
an engine that supports pipelining has been loaded, and max_pipelines is
greater than 1. See
.Xr SSL_CTX_set_split_send_fragment 3
for further information.
.It Sy -max_pipelines Em +int
The maximum number of encrypt/decrypt pipelines to be used.
This will only have an effect if an engine has been loaded that supports
pipelining (e.g.
the dasync engine) and a suitable cipher suite has been negotiated.
The default value is 1. See
.Xr SSL_CTX_set_max_pipelines 3
for further information.
.It Sy -naccept Em +int
The server will exit after receiving the specified number of
connections, default unlimited.
.It Sy -read_buf Em +int
The default read buffer size to be used for connections.
This will only have an effect if the buffer size is larger than the size
that would otherwise be used and pipelining is in use (see
.Xr SSL_CTX_set_default_read_buffer_len 3
for further information).
.It Sy -no_tx_cert_comp
Disables support for sending TLSv1.3 compressed certificates.
.It Sy -no_rx_cert_comp
Disables support for receiving TLSv1.3 compressed certificates.
.It Sy -no_comp
Disable negotiation of TLS compression.
TLS compression is not recommended and is off by default as of OpenSSL
1.1.0.
.It Sy -num_tickets
Control the number of tickets that will be sent to the client after a
full handshake in TLSv1.3. The default number of tickets is 2. This
option does not affect the number of tickets sent after a resumption
handshake.
.It Sy -dhparam Em infile
The DH parameter file to use.
The ephemeral DH cipher suites generate keys using a set of DH
parameters.
If not specified then an attempt is made to load the parameters from the
server certificate file.
If this fails then a static set of parameters hard coded into this
command will be used.
.It Sy -nbio
Turns on non blocking I/O.
.It Sy -timeout
Enable timeouts.
.It Sy -mtu
Set link-layer MTU.
.It Sy -psk_identity Em val
Expect the client to send PSK identity
.Em val
when using a PSK cipher suite, and warn if they do not.
By default, the expected PSK identity is the string "Client_identity".
.It Sy -psk_hint Em val
Use the PSK identity hint
.Em val
when using a PSK cipher suite.
.It Sy -psk Em val
Use the PSK key
.Em val
when using a PSK cipher suite.
The key is given as a hexadecimal number without leading 0x, for example
-psk 1a2b3c4d.
This option must be provided in order to use a PSK cipher.
.It Sy -psk_session Em file
Use the pem encoded SSL_SESSION data stored in
.Em file
as the basis of a PSK. Note that this will only work if TLSv1.3 is
negotiated.
.It Sy -srpvfile
The verifier file for SRP. This option is deprecated.
.It Sy -srpuserseed
A seed string for a default user salt.
This option is deprecated.
.It Sy -listen
This option can only be used in conjunction with one of the DTLS options
above.
With this option, this command will listen on a UDP port for incoming
connections.
Any ClientHellos that arrive will be checked to see if they have a
cookie in them or not.
Any without a cookie will be responded to with a HelloVerifyRequest.
If a ClientHello with a cookie is received then this command will
connect to that peer and complete the handshake.
.It Sy -sctp
Use SCTP for the transport protocol instead of UDP in DTLS. Must be used
in conjunction with
.Sy -dtls ,
.Sy -dtls1
or
.Sy -dtls1_2 .
This option is only available where OpenSSL has support for SCTP
enabled.
.It Sy -sctp_label_bug
Use the incorrect behaviour of older OpenSSL implementations when
computing endpoint-pair shared secrets for DTLS/SCTP. This allows
communication with older broken implementations but breaks
interoperability with correct implementations.
Must be used in conjunction with
.Sy -sctp .
This option is only available where OpenSSL has support for SCTP
enabled.
.It Sy -use_srtp
Offer SRTP key management with a colon-separated profile list.
.It Sy -no_dhe
If this option is set then no DH parameters will be loaded effectively
disabling the ephemeral DH cipher suites.
.It Sy -alpn Em val Ns , Sy -nextprotoneg Em val
These flags enable the Application-Layer Protocol Negotiation or Next
Protocol Negotiation (NPN) extension, respectively.
ALPN is the IETF standard and replaces NPN. The
.Em val
list is a comma-separated list of supported protocol names.
The list should contain the most desirable protocols first.
Protocol names are printable ASCII strings, for example "http/1.1" or
"spdy/3". The flag
.Sy -nextprotoneg
cannot be specified if
.Sy -tls1_3
is used.
.It Sy -ktls
Enable Kernel TLS for sending and receiving.
This option was introduced in OpenSSL 3.2.0. Kernel TLS is off by
default as of OpenSSL 3.2.0.
.It Sy -sendfile
If this option is set and KTLS is enabled,
.Xr SSL_sendfile 3
will be used instead of
.Xr BIO_write 3
to send the HTTP response requested by a client.
This option is only valid when
.Sy -ktls
along with
.Sy -WWW
or
.Sy -HTTP
are specified.
.It Sy -zerocopy_sendfile
If this option is set,
.Xr SSL_sendfile 3
will use the zerocopy TX mode, which gives a performance boost when used
with KTLS hardware offload.
Note that invalid TLS records might be transmitted if the file is
changed while being sent.
This option depends on
.Sy -sendfile ;
when used alone,
.Sy -sendfile
is implied, and a warning is shown.
Note that KTLS sendfile on FreeBSD always runs in the zerocopy mode.
.It Sy -keylogfile Em outfile
Appends TLS secrets to the specified keylog file such that external
programs (like Wireshark) can decrypt TLS connections.
.It Sy -max_early_data Em int
Change the default maximum early data bytes that are specified for new
sessions and any incoming early data (when used in conjunction with the
.Sy -early_data
flag).
The default value is approximately 16k.
The argument must be an integer greater than or equal to 0.
.It Sy -recv_max_early_data Em int
Specify the hard limit on the maximum number of early data bytes that
will be accepted.
.It Sy -early_data
Accept early data where possible.
Cannot be used in conjunction with
.Sy -www ,
.Sy -WWW ,
.Sy -HTTP
or
.Sy -rev .
.It Sy -stateless
Require TLSv1.3 cookies.
.It Sy -anti_replay Ns , Sy -no_anti_replay
Switches replay protection on or off, respectively.
Replay protection is on by default unless overridden by a configuration
file.
When it is on, OpenSSL will automatically detect if a session ticket has
been used more than once, TLSv1.3 has been negotiated, and early data is
enabled on the server.
A full handshake is forced if a session ticket is used a second or
subsequent time.
Any early data that was sent will be rejected.
.It Sy -tfo
Enable acceptance of TCP Fast Open (RFC7413) connections.
.It Sy -cert_comp
Pre-compresses certificates (RFC8879) that will be sent during the
handshake.
.It Sy -nameopt Em option
This specifies how the subject or issuer names are displayed.
See
.Xr openssl-namedisplay-options 1
for details.
.It Sy -no_ssl3 Ns , Sy -no_tls1 Ns , Sy -no_tls1_1 Ns , Sy -no_tls1_2 Ns , Sy -no_tls1_3 Ns , Sy -ssl3 Ns , Sy -tls1 Ns , Sy -tls1_1 Ns , Sy -tls1_2 Ns , Sy -tls1_3
See
.Xr openssl 1 .
.It Sy -dtls Ns , Sy -dtls1 Ns , Sy -dtls1_2
These specify the use of DTLS instead of TLS. See
.Xr openssl 1 .
.It Sy -bugs Ns , Sy -comp Ns , Sy -no_comp Ns , Sy -no_ticket Ns , Sy -serverpref Ns , Sy -client_renegotiation Ns , Sy -legacy_renegotiation Ns , Sy -no_renegotiation Ns , Sy -no_resumption_on_reneg Ns , Sy -legacy_server_connect Ns , Sy -no_legacy_server_connect Ns , Sy -no_etm Sy -allow_no_dhe_kex Ns , Sy -prefer_no_dhe_kex Ns , Sy -prioritize_chacha Ns , Sy -strict Ns , Sy -sigalgs Em algs Ns , Sy -client_sigalgs Em algs Ns , Sy -groups Em groups Ns , Sy -curves Em curves Ns , Sy -named_curve Em curve Ns , Sy -cipher Em ciphers Ns , Sy -ciphersuites Em 1.3ciphers Ns , Sy -min_protocol Em minprot Ns , Sy -max_protocol Em maxprot Ns , Sy -record_padding Em padding Ns , Sy -debug_broken_protocol Ns , Sy -no_middlebox
See
.Xr SSL_CONF_cmd 3
for details.
.It Sy -xkey Em infile Ns , Sy -xcert Em file Ns , Sy -xchain Em file Ns , Sy -xchain_build Em file Ns , Sy -xcertform Sy DER Ns | Ns Sy PEM Ns , Sy -xkeyform Sy DER Ns | Ns Sy PEM
Set extended certificate verification options.
See
.Xr openssl-verification-options 1
for details.
.It Sy -CAfile Em file Ns , Sy -no-CAfile Ns , Sy -CApath Em dir Ns , Sy -no-CApath Ns , Sy -CAstore Em uri Ns , Sy -no-CAstore
See
.Xr openssl-verification-options 1
for details.
.It Sy -rand Em files Ns , Sy -writerand Em file
See
.Xr openssl 1
for details.
.It Sy -engine Em id
See
.Xr openssl 1 .
This option is deprecated.
.It Sy -provider Em name
.It Sy -provider-path Em path
.It Sy -provparam Em [name:]key=value
.It Sy -propquery Em propq
See
.Xr openssl 1 ,
.Xr provider 7 ,
and
.Xr property 7 .
.It Sy -allow_proxy_certs Ns , Sy -attime Ns , Sy -no_check_time Ns , Sy -check_ss_sig Ns , Sy -crl_check Ns , Sy -crl_check_all Ns , Sy -explicit_policy Ns , Sy -extended_crl Ns , Sy -ignore_critical Ns , Sy -inhibit_any Ns , Sy -inhibit_map Ns , Sy -no_alt_chains Ns , Sy -partial_chain Ns , Sy -policy Ns , Sy -policy_check Ns , Sy -policy_print Ns , Sy -purpose Ns , Sy -suiteB_128 Ns , Sy -suiteB_128_only Ns , Sy -suiteB_192 Ns , Sy -trusted_first Ns , Sy -use_deltas Ns , Sy -auth_level Ns , Sy -verify_depth Ns , Sy -verify_email Ns , Sy -verify_hostname Ns , Sy -verify_ip Ns , Sy -verify_name Ns , Sy -x509_strict Sy -issuer_checks
Set various options of certificate chain verification.
See
.Xr openssl-verification-options 1
for details.
.Pp
If the server requests a client certificate, then verification errors
are displayed, for debugging, but the command will proceed unless the
.Sy -verify_return_error
option is used.
.It Sy -enable_server_rpk
Enable support for sending raw public keys (RFC7250) to the client.
A raw public key will be sent by the server, if solicited by the client,
provided a suitable key and public certificate pair is configured.
Clients that don't support raw public keys or prefer to use X.509
certificates can still elect to receive X.509 certificates as usual.
.Pp
Raw public keys are extracted from the configured certificate/private
key.
.It Sy -enable_client_rpk
Enable support for receiving raw public keys (RFC7250) from the client.
Use of X.509 certificates by the client becomes optional, and clients
that support raw public keys may elect to use them.
Clients that don't support raw public keys or prefer to use X.509
certificates can still elect to send X.509 certificates as usual.
.Pp
Raw public keys are extracted from the configured certificate/private
key.
.El
.Sh CONNECTED COMMANDS
If a connection request is established with an SSL client and neither
the
.Sy -www
nor the
.Sy -WWW
option has been used then normally any data received from the client is
displayed and any key presses will be sent to the client.
.Pp
Certain commands are also recognized which perform special operations.
These commands are a letter which must appear at the start of a line.
They are listed below.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Sy q
End the current SSL connection but still accept new connections.
.It Sy Q
End the current SSL connection and exit.
.It Sy r
Renegotiate the SSL session (TLSv1.2 and below only).
.It Sy R
Renegotiate the SSL session and request a client certificate (TLSv1.2
and below only).
.It Sy P
Send some plain text down the underlying TCP connection: this should
cause the client to disconnect due to a protocol violation.
.It Sy S
Print out some session cache status information.
.It Sy k
Send a key update message to the client (TLSv1.3 only)
.It Sy K
Send a key update message to the client and request one back (TLSv1.3
only)
.It Sy c
Send a certificate request to the client (TLSv1.3 only)
.El
.Sh NOTES
This command can be used to debug SSL clients.
To accept connections from a web browser the command:
.Bd -literal
 openssl s_server -accept 443 -www
.Ed
.Pp
can be used for example.
.Pp
Although specifying an empty list of CAs when requesting a client
certificate is strictly speaking a protocol violation, some SSL clients
interpret this to mean any CA is acceptable.
This is useful for debugging purposes.
.Pp
The session parameters can printed out using the
.Xr openssl-sess_id 1
command.
.Sh BUGS
Because this program has a lot of options and also because some of the
techniques used are rather old, the C source for this command is rather
hard to read and not a model of how things should be done.
A typical SSL server program would be much simpler.
.Pp
The output of common ciphers is wrong: it just gives the list of ciphers
that OpenSSL recognizes and the client supports.
.Pp
There should be a way for this command to print out details of any
unknown cipher suites a client says it supports.
